> The secret to Kao Yu lies in two stages: first, get your wok screaming hot to achieve that beautiful charred crust on the fish. Then finish it in a rich, spicy broth that infuses everything with depth. The contrast of textures—that crisp exterior against silky broth—makes this dish extraordinary.

== Prepare the Fish ==

Have your fishmonger butterfly the @whole fish{900%g} (scaled, gutted, and cleaned). Choose a flaky white fish like tilapia, catfish, bass, or branzino. When you get home, remove the black membrane from the belly cavity—这一步很重要 (this step is important) as it can give the fish a bitter taste.

Pat the fish thoroughly dry with paper towels. This is crucial—any moisture on the surface will steam instead of char. Rub all over with @Shaoxing wine{15%ml}, @salt{3%g}, and @white pepper{2%g}. Let marinate for ~{30%minutes}.

== Prepare the Vegetables ==

Soak @dried bean threads{60%g} (also called bean curd sticks or foo jook) until pliable, then cut into 5 cm pieces.

While the bean threads soak, prepare your vegetables and set them aside on a plate:
- @celery{85%g} — thinly sliced on an angle into bite-sized pieces
- @mushrooms{85%g} — wood ear, enoki, beech, oyster, king trumpet, or shiitake (each brings different texture)
- @lotus root{170%g} — peeled and thinly sliced (look for pre-sliced at Asian grocers to save time)
- @soybean sprouts{115%g} — rinsed and drained

== Char the Fish ==

This is the make-or-break step. Place your #wok{} over high heat and let it heat until it just begins to smoke—seriously, let it get ripping hot. A properly preheated wok prevents sticking and creates that sought-after char.

Add @neutral oil{45%ml} and swirl to coat. Carefully lay the butterflied fish skin-side down. Resist the urge to move it—let it fry undisturbed for ~{5-6%minutes} until golden. Rotate the wok occasionally for even browning.

Shake gently—the fish should release on its own when ready. If it sticks, wait another minute. Flip carefully using two spatulas and brown the other side, ~{3%minutes}. The fish is done when the flesh flakes easily from the center bone and both sides are deeply golden.

Move to a large plate and set aside. You should have a thin film of oil remaining in the wok.

== Build the Sauce ==

Reduce heat to medium-high. Add another drizzle of @neutral oil{15%ml} if needed. Add your aromatics:
- @ginger{10%g} — 6 slices, thinly sliced (not minced—larger pieces won't burn)
- @garlic{45%g} — 15 cloves, smashed (releases more flavor than whole)
- @dried chilies{10%g} — for heat (omit if sensitive)
- @Sichuan peppercorns{10%g} — for that signature numbing flavor (omit if unavailable)
- @bay leaves{6}
- @star anise pods{3}

Sauté for ~{2%minutes} until intensely fragrant.

Push the aromatics aside and add @spicy bean sauce{60%ml} (辣豆瓣酱, also labeled as spicy broad bean paste). Let the oil turn red from the sauce before stirring—this frying technique is called "zao you" and develops deep flavor. Adjust heat as needed to prevent burning.

Pour in @Shaoxing wine{15%ml} to deglaze, then add @chicken stock{960%ml} and @sugar{10%g}. Bring to a vigorous boil.

== Finish with Vegetables ==

Add the bean threads, celery, mushrooms, and lotus root. Cook until they're crisp-tender—you want a slight bite remaining. This takes roughly 3-4 minutes depending on your vegetable choices.

Toss in the soybean sprouts in the final minute of cooking. Season with @salt{to taste}.

== Plate and Serve ==

Pour everything directly into a large, wide serving bowl or shallow dish—something that accommodates both the broth and fish. Nestle the charred fish on top. The broth should surround but not drown the fish.

Scatter @scallions{30%g} and/or @cilantro{30%g} over the top. Serve immediately while bubbling, spooning the fragrant sauce over the fish with each bite.